# The compilers this commit expects to be answered by.
#
# ## Why this file exists when `flake.lock` already pins them
#
# It is NOT a second pin, and it must not be read as one. `flake.lock` is the
# pin: the Nim compiler in the dev shell is `codetracer-toolchains`'
# `packages.nim-2_2` (`nix/packages/default.nix`'s `nim-codetracer`), and
# `rustc`/`cargo` are `fenix`'s stable toolchain combined in
# `nix/shells/ci-base.nix`. Both are root inputs of `flake.nix` with 40-hex
# locked revs, so the commit ALREADY determines them.
#
# What the commit did not have was any way to state, cheaply and without
# evaluating the flake, WHICH COMPILER IT EXPECTS — so nothing could tell a
# build that answered with a different one. That gap is not hypothetical:
#
#   * `nim` outside the dev shell on this workstation is 2.2.4 from
#     `~/.nix-profile/bin/nim`; inside it is 2.2.8. Both are `/nix/store`
#     paths after symlink resolution, so "is it from the store" does not
#     separate them.
#   * A nine-hour investigation into a renderer "build break" ended at one
#     machine's compiler, not at the source.
#   * Two builds that both had all three sibling repositories at their pins
#     differed by 60,920 bytes (`scripts/sibling-pins.sh`'s header records it).
#
# So this file is a REDUNDANT RESTATEMENT of what `flake.lock` implies, kept
# for exactly one property: drift becomes a DIFF. A toolchain bump now has to
# edit a tracked file, and a reviewer sees "the compiler changed" in the same
# patch as whatever number moved because of it.
#
# ## How the redundancy is kept from rotting
#
# Every `LOCK_*` line below records the `flake.lock` revision the `EXPECT_*`
# lines were MEASURED under. `scripts/toolchain-pins.sh --verify` compares them
# against `flake.lock` and FAILS when they disagree, with "re-measure" as the
# remedy. A restatement that could silently lag the thing it restates would be
# worse than no restatement at all — it would assert a compiler nobody ships,
# which is the entire defect class this file belongs to.
#
# ## TO BUMP
#
#   1. Update `flake.lock` (`nix flake update codetracer-toolchains` / `fenix`).
#   2. Enter the dev shell and run `bash scripts/toolchain-pins.sh --stamp`.
#   3. Copy the measured versions and the new revs here, in one commit with
#      whatever the bump moved.

# The host the versions below were measured on. Version strings are not
# expected to differ by platform (both toolchains are pinned by revision, not
# by channel), so this is provenance for the reader rather than a key — but if
# a version ever DOES differ by platform, this line is where the reader learns
# which one was written down.
TOOLCHAIN_HOST_MEASURED=aarch64-darwin
TOOLCHAIN_MEASURED_ON=2026-09-02

# --- Nim --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# From `codetracer-toolchains`' `nim-2_2`, wrapped as `nim-codetracer` in
# `nix/packages/default.nix`. Provides both `nim` and `nim2`.
EXPECT_NIM_VERSION=2.2.8

# UPSTREAM, and this was MEASURED rather than assumed, because the opposite was
# the standing hypothesis and it was wrong. `flake.lock` does carry a
# `nim-fork-src` node — `git+https://github.com/metacraft-labs/nim`, ref
# `codetracer`, rev 0b5b5ec507d2d9c731d222184c625851377a02c8 — and the obvious
# reading is that the dev shell's Nim is that fork. It is not. `nim-fork-src`
# is an input of `reprobuild` and of nothing else, so it builds `repro`, not
# CodeTracer. The dev shell's compiler resolves to a derivation whose `src` is
#
#     https://nim-lang.org/download/nim-2.2.8.tar.xz
#     sha256-EUGRr6CDxQWdy+XOiNvk9CVCz/BOLDAXZo7kOLwLjPw=
#
# reached by `nix-store -q --deriver $(command -v nim)` and then
# `nix derivation show` on the `.drv` and on its `src` input. A guess would
# have been recorded here as a gap; this is a measurement, so it is recorded as
# a value, and `--verify` re-checks the REACHABILITY half of it (that
# `nim-fork-src` has not become reachable from `codetracer-toolchains`) on
# every run.
EXPECT_NIM_SOURCE=upstream

# --- Rust -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `fenixPkgs.stable` combined with the wasm32/x86_64 rust-std targets in
# `nix/shells/ci-base.nix`; the combined derivation is named `rust-mixed`.
# The parenthesised hash in `rustc --version` is rustc's OWN upstream commit
# and is checked as part of the version string, because it is the one identity
# rustc self-reports.
EXPECT_RUSTC_VERSION=1.96.0
EXPECT_RUSTC_COMMIT=ac68faa20

# --- nargo: DELIBERATELY ABSENT ---------------------------------------------
#
# There is no EXPECT_NARGO_VERSION, and its absence is a decision rather than
# an omission. In a workspace dev shell `nargo` is NOT the flake's nargo:
# `scripts/detect-siblings.sh` prepends `../noir/target/release` to PATH when
# that binary exists, so what answers is a release build of the `noir` SIBLING
# WORKING COPY. Recording an expected version would therefore encode one
# developer's checkout as the project's expectation and would fail for
# everyone else.
#
# `nargo` is instead verified STRUCTURALLY, against its own checkout, by
# `scripts/toolchain-pins.sh`: the binary self-reports its build revision
# (`git version hash: <sha>, is dirty: <bool>`), so the guard can require that
# revision to be the checkout's HEAD and the checkout to be clean. That is the
# check that catches the real failure — a binary built weeks ago from a commit
# the working copy has since moved past, which is what was on this machine when
# this file was written (binary at 906af2f4, checkout HEAD five commits later).
#
# The `noir` flake pin is recorded below all the same, because the divergence
# between it and the sibling is worth REPORTING even where it is not worth
# failing on. See the guard's header for why that split is drawn where it is.

# --- The flake.lock revisions the above were measured under -----------------
#
# `scripts/toolchain-pins.sh --verify` fails when any of these disagrees with
# `flake.lock`. Do not "fix" a mismatch by editing these lines alone: they are
# a claim that the EXPECT_* values above were observed at these revisions, and
# editing them without re-measuring makes that claim false.
LOCK_CODETRACER_TOOLCHAINS=942c995a36469853351af605da90025314ffc58e
LOCK_FENIX=dd2c80d0b88463ccc0402c86e9e72dbb354ac091
LOCK_NOIR=5e98f904cde508d574ba559da692a312476ff032
